      "reference": "9.15",
      "verdict": "blocked",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": false,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "πόσοις κατ’ ὄναρ ὁμοίως ἄλλοις ὤφθησαν καὶ ὕπαρ συναντήσαντες ἀλλήλοις πρὸς τὸ κατ’ ὄναρ ἀντιβάλλοντες οὐ συνεφώνησαν;",
          "english_quote": "To how many other people have they similarly appeared in dreams, and, when those people met one another while awake and compared what they had seen in dreams, did not agree?",
          "issue": "The concluding “did not agree?” has no expressed subject in its English clause. Folding it into the opening “to how many” also obscures the negative rhetorical question: the English can suggest counting people who failed to agree rather than asking whether the recipients did not agree with one another.",
          "suggested_revision": "A clearer contextual rendering is: “To how many other people have they similarly appeared in dreams? And when those people met one another while awake and compared their dream experiences, did they not agree?” Explain that the people receiving the appearances are understood as the subsequent participants in the comparison and that dividing the question is interpretive. Resubmit the replacement for review.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τοῖς παρίᾳ φόβοις",
          "english_quote": "the fears and desire that arise",
          "issue": "No defensible contribution of the supplied παρίᾳ is represented in the English. The existing source_issues note honestly acknowledges this omission, but disclosure cannot supply the missing meaning. The print is already image-verified; the remaining block concerns the unresolved lexical and grammatical account of the printed wording, not unreadable ink or ordinary interpretive alternatives.",
          "suggested_revision": "Obtain a documented lexical and syntactic explanation of παρίᾳ in this construction, or evidence supporting an explicitly identified proposed correction distinct from the supplied reading. Do not silently omit or replace the word, and do not introduce a dotted lacuna absent from the source. The entire affected clause must then undergo fresh review.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἢ ψυχῆς τὰ ἐπιγενήματα τοῖς παρίᾳ φόβοις καὶ ἐπιθυμίᾳ ἀποδιδούσης τὰς ἰδέας",
          "english_quote": "or the soul's, giving images in response to the fears and desire that arise.",
          "issue": "The substantival phrase τὰ ἐπιγενήματα is not adequately represented. The draft instead attaches “that arise” to “fears and desire,” assigning the arising to those nouns without establishing that attachment from the Greek. This is an additional problem beyond the acknowledged omission of παρίᾳ.",
          "suggested_revision": "Reanalyse the whole construction and explicitly account for τὰ ἐπιγενήματα, for which “the ensuing effects/experiences” is a possible lexical starting point. Explain its syntactic relationship to ἀποδιδούσης τὰς ἰδέας. Do not treat that provisional gloss as an accepted reconstruction or repair the clause merely by making the fears and desire “arise.”",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἢ γὰρ φόβῳ τὸν νοῦν πληγεῖσα, διὰ ὀνείρων τὰς ἰδέας ἀποκυΐσκει.",
          "english_quote": "For, struck in mind by fear, it brings forth images through dreams.",
          "issue": "The initial disjunctive ἢ is omitted. Retaining only “For” suppresses the source's alternative connection with the preceding explanation.",
          "suggested_revision": "Represent both the disjunctive ἢ and explanatory γάρ, retaining the source's abrupt connection if necessary, and explain their relationship to the preceding alternatives in a note. Do not silently reduce ἢ γάρ to γάρ alone.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "9.18",
